1. Introduction
Asymptotic analysis is used to study how the running time of an algorithm increases when the input size becomes very large.

The three algorithms used for locating student registration numbers are:
1. Linear Search
2. Recursive Binary Search
3. Hash Table Search
The notations used are:
- Big-O (O): Represents the upper bound or worst-case growth.
- Omega (Ω): Represents the lower bound or best-case growth.
- Theta (Θ): Represents the tight bound when the upper and lower bounds have the same growth rate.
2. Linear Search – Asymptotic Analysis
Linear Search checks registration numbers one by one until the target is found.

Best Case:

When the target is in the first position, only one comparison is required.
\[
\Omega(1)
\]

Worst Case:

When the target is in the last position or is absent, all n elements are checked.
\[
O(n)
\]

Average Case:

The average number of comparisons is:
\[
C(n)=\frac{n+1}{2}
\]

Ignoring constants and smaller terms:
\[
\Theta(n)
\]

Interpretation:

When the dataset increases from 1,000 to 1,000,000 registration numbers, the worst-case number of comparisons increases from 1,000 to 1,000,000.

Therefore, Linear Search becomes slower for very large datasets because its work increases directly with the number of records.
3. Recursive Binary Search – Asymptotic Analysis
Binary Search divides the sorted registration numbers into two halves after every comparison.

Best Case:

The target is found at the middle position in the first comparison.
\[
\Omega(1)
\]

Worst Case:

The search area is repeatedly divided into two halves.
\[
O(\log_2 n)
\]

Average Case:

The number of comparisons also grows logarithmically.
\[
\Theta(\log n)
\]

Interpretation:

For 1,000 records:
\[
\log_2 1000\approx 10
\]

For 1,000,000 records:
\[
\log_2 1000000\approx 20
\]

Even though the dataset increases 1,000 times, the number of comparisons increases only from approximately 10 to 20.

Binary Search is therefore highly efficient for large datasets, provided the registration numbers are sorted.

Preprocessing consideration:

If sorting is required, Merge Sort takes:
\[
O(n\log n)
\]

Thus, the total cost of sorting and one search is:
\[
O(n\log n)+O(\log n)=O(n\log n)
\]

If the data is already sorted, searching alone takes \(O(\log n)\).
4. Hash Table Search – Asymptotic Analysis
Hash Table Search calculates an index using a hash function and searches the corresponding bucket.

Best Case:

The target is the first element in the selected bucket.
\[
\Omega(1)
\]

Average Case:

When the hash function distributes registration numbers evenly and the load factor is controlled:
\[
\Theta(1)
\]

Worst Case:

When all registration numbers produce the same hash index, the algorithm may check every element.
\[
O(n)
\]

Interpretation:

For a well-designed hash table, searching 1,000 registration numbers and searching 1,000,000 registration numbers can both require approximately constant average search time.

However, collisions and poor hash distribution can increase the number of comparisons.

Preprocessing consideration:

Building the hash table requires inserting all registration numbers.
\[
P(n)=O(n)
\]

Therefore, the total cost of building the table and performing one search is:
\[
O(n)+O(1)=O(n)
\]

When the hash table is already built, each average search takes approximately \(O(1)\).

5. Overall Asymptotic Comparison
Algorithm	Best Case	Average Case	Worst Case
Linear Search	Ω(1)	Θ(n)	O(n)
Binary Search	Ω(1)	Θ(log n)	O(log n)
Hash Table Search	Ω(1)	Θ(1)	O(n)

